Nice little Pythag bash :)

<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let the point of tangency of  $\overline{EF}$  with the circle be  $D.$  

Since the diameter of the circle has length  $10,$  we have   $$ OA=OB=OD=5. $$  Since   $$ \angle{OBF}=\angle{BFD}=\angle{FDO}=90^\circ, $$   it follows that  $OBFD$  is a square.

Hence,   $$ OB=BF=FD=OD=5. $$  By the Pythagorean Theorem, we have
\begin{align*}
BO^2+BC^2=OC^2 \implies BC &= \sqrt{OC^2-BO^2} 
&= \sqrt{13^2-5^2} 
&= \sqrt{169-25} 
&= \sqrt{144} 
&=12.
\end{align*}
Hence,
\begin{align*}
FC&=BC-BF 
&= 12-5 
&= 7.
\end{align*}
Let   $$ AE=x. $$  Then, we have   $$ ED=x, EC=12-x, $$   so,   $$ FE=ED+DF=x+5. $$   Therefore, by the Pythagorean Theorem,
\begin{align*}
FC^2+FE^2 = EC^2 &\implies 7^2+(x+5)^2=(12-x)^2 
&\implies 49+x^2+10x+25=x^2-24x+144 
&\implies 34x=70 
&\implies x = \frac{70}{34}=\frac{35}{17}.
\end{align*}
Thus,
\begin{align*}
EF &= x+5 
&= \frac{35}{17}+5 
&= \frac{120}{17},
\end{align*}
so the answer is
\begin{align*}
\frac{EF\cdot FC}{2} &= \frac{\frac{120}{17}\cdot7}{2} 
&= \frac{\frac{840}{17}}{2} 
&= \boxed{\frac{420}{17}}.
\end{align*}</details>

This was one of my more favorite problems I wrote for the round. I'm surprised there's no solution yet, so I'll include one.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>We have
\[\left\{\begin{array}{ccc}
a_1+a_2 & = & b_1+b_2
a_1+a_2+a_3 & = & b_1+b_2+b_3\end{array}\right\| \implies a_3=b_3,\]\[\left\{\begin{array}{ccc}
a_3 & = & b_3
a_3+a_4+a_5 & = & b_3+b_4+b_5 \end{array}\right\| \implies a_4+a_5=b_4+b_5,\]\[\left\{\begin{array}{ccc}
a_4+a_5 & = & b_4+b_5
a_4+a_5+a_6 & = & b_4+b_5+b_6\end{array}\right\| \implies a_6=b_6,\]and continuing in this manner reveals that  $a_{3k}=b_{3k}$  for all positive integers  $k$ . Remark that
\[\left\{\begin{array}{ccc}
a_{n-1}+a_n & = & b_{n-1}+b_n
a_{n-2}+a_{n-1}+a_n & = & b_{n-2}+b_{n-1}+b_n\end{array}\right\| \implies a_{n-2}=b_{n-2}.\]We claim that the junk positive integers are exactly the ones which leave a remainder of  $2$  when divided by  $3$ . Suppose  $n$  does not leave a remainder of  $2$  when divided by  $3$ . Then either  $n-1$  or  $n$  is a multiple of  $3$ , hence either  $a_{n-1}=b_{n-1}$  or  $a_n=b_n$ . However, we also have  $a_{n-2}=b_{n-2}$  and  $a_{n-2}+a_{n-1}+a_n  =  b_{n-2}+b_{n-1}+b_n$ , thus it follows that  $a_n=b_n$  and  $a_{n-1}=b_{n-1}$ . Now since  $a_{i-1}+a_i+a_{i+1}=b_{i-1}+b_i+b_{i+1}$ , it's easy to see that we will have  $a_i=b_i$  for all  $i$ , which contradicts the binary string's distinctness. It remains to give an example of suitable strings for  $n\equiv 2\pmod{3}$ . We can take
\begin{align*} a_1a_2\cdots a_n &=\,01\underbrace{\, 001\, 001\cdots 001}_{\frac{n-2}{3}\, 001s} 
b_1b_2\cdots b_n &=\,10\underbrace{\, 010\, 010\cdots 010}_{\frac{n-2}{3}\, 010s}
\end{align*}hence the claim is proven. The number of  $2\pmod{3}$  positive integers less than or equal to  $2016$  is  $\frac{2016}{3}=672$ .</details>

